{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>You shall not eat any carcass, etc.</b> Its saying <b>kol</b> — according to the one who says a prohibition takes effect upon [another] prohibition (Ḥullin 113a) — intends to include everything that becomes a carcass, whether of pure animals or impure animals; upon them all comes the warning ״לֹא תֹאכְלוּ כָל נְבֵלָה״. And according to the one who says a prohibition does not take effect upon [another] prohibition, its saying <b>kol</b> includes every species of pure animal, whether domesticated animal, wild animal, or fowl — but an impure one, no." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend by saying <b>kol</b>, based on what they said in the Mishnah (Me'ilah 4:3) and which the Rambam ruled in chapter 4 of the Laws of Forbidden Foods — and this is its language: all carcasses combine with one another, etc. How so? One who takes from the carcass of an ox and the carcass of a deer and the carcass of a rooster and gathers from all of them an olive's-bulk of meat and eats it — receives lashes, end quote. And that is the statement of <b>לֹא תֹאכְלוּ כָל נְבֵלָה</b>, meaning that it warns concerning the measure of eating, which is an olive's-bulk that comes from anything that becomes a carcass, even though the olive's-bulk does not come from any one of them alone but only from all three together." "<b>To the ger who, etc.</b> It placed the mention of the ger before the mention of giving, and in the case of the nokhri it placed the mention of selling before the mention of the nokhri. In tractate Ḥullin (Ḥullin 114a) they brought a dispute between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Yehudah. Rabbi Meir holds that the verse came to speak in this order to permit giving to a gentile and selling to a ger, and therefore it placed giving and selling adjacent. And Rabbi Yehudah holds that the words are as written, because the verse said ״אוֹ מָכֹר״ and did not say ״וּמָכֹר״. And Rabbi Meir explains the word ״אוֹ״ as serving to place giving to the ger before selling to the gentile. And one needs to know — according to the reasoning of Rabbi Yehudah — why the verse spoke in this order. And it may be explained based on another dispute that they disagreed about in tractate Avodah Zarah (Avodah Zarah 67b), between Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Shimon regarding the law of a blemished [prohibition]: Rabbi Shimon permits and Rabbi Meir prohibits. For it was taught: ״לֹא תֹאכְלוּ כָל נְבֵלָה לַגֵּר אֲשֶׁר״ etc. — anything fit for a ger is called a carcass; that which is not fit for a ger is not called a carcass, end quote. This implies that Rabbi Shimon's derivation is from the fact that the verse came to say to give it to a ger, and if it is not fit, a person does not give and sell something that has no value. From this you have learned that the verse speaks of what is fit, and therefore he derives that it is called a carcass only if [it is fit], etc. But these words do not appear [correct], for whenever the verse forbids a carcass — every carcass is implied; and the statement of giving and selling comes when they are fit. And who suspended one upon the other?" "<b>Therefore</b> it appears that Rabbi Shimon's derivation is from the fact that the verse altered the arrangement of expression and wrote the word ״לַגֵּר״ adjacent to the word ״נְבֵלָה״, and did not say it in the order it said afterward: ״מָכֹר לְנָכְרִי״ — which would also be the proper arrangement of expression, to present the action first and afterward say to whom. Therefore Rabbi Shimon derived that the verse came to alert you concerning this point, to say that it is a carcass only if it is fit for a ger; but if the verse had said ״תִּתְּנֶנָּה לַגֵּר״, there would be no place for his derivation." "<b>And according to</b> this approach we have found the reason for the change of order according to Rabbi Yehudah, for he holds like Rabbi Shimon that there is no [additional] prohibition of a blemished [animal], and for this reason the verse altered its arrangement to speak in this order, to place the word ״נְבֵלָה״ adjacent to the word ״הַגֵּר״, and it deviated from the order adjacent to it in order to alert you to this derivation. And therefore for Rabbi Meir, who derives that the verse came to permit giving to a gentile and selling to a ger, there is no place for him to derive ״a carcass fit for a ger״ etc., and he prohibits even if it is not fit for a ger — and their words are settled according to their reasoning." "<b>Rather,</b> I saw there in tractate Avodah Zarah (68a) that the Talmud raises a difficulty regarding when it speaks of a carcass (nevelah) that is not fit for a ger — it is not [technically] a nevelah. It raised the difficulty, and this is its language: ״And Rabbi Meir — that [verse] comes to exclude an animal that was a terefah from the outset (seruchah me-ikarah). And Rabbi Shimon — an animal that was a terefah from the outset does not need a verse,״ end quote. Now according to our words, what is the Talmud's difficulty to Rabbi Meir? For according to Rabbi Meir's reasoning there is no place to derive Rabbi Shimon's derivation, as we have written? Perhaps it is for the breadth of the matter (leravcha d'milta) that the Gemara said thus — even if you were to say that he holds Rabbi Shimon's derivation could be applied to an animal that was a terefah from the outset, yet according to the truth there is no difficulty for Rabbi Meir, as we have written. And according to this, according to the truth Rabbi Meir too would hold (like Rabbi Shimon) that an animal that was a terefah from the outset is mere dust (afra b'alma), according to the opinion of Rashbi, and does not need a verse."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {5 "The comment opens with אֶלָּא ('rather'), continuing from a preceding discussion not included here. The Talmudic passage cited (b. Avodah Zarah 68a) discusses whether נְבֵלָה שֶׁאֵינָהּ רְאוּיָה לַגֵּר retains the halakhic status of nevelah; סְרוּחָה מֵעִיקָרָא refers to an animal with a pre-existing condition (tereifah) that would have rendered it forbidden even before death. 'כרבי שמעון' in parentheses appears in the source text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:57.168675777Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}